### What is the Redundant Connection problem?

The problem gives a graph that was originally a tree of n nodes (connected, no cycles) with exactly one extra edge added, creating a single cycle. The task is to find and return that extra edge. If there are multiple candidates, return the last one in input order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RedundantConnection {
    private int[] parent;
    private int[] rank;

    public int[] findRedundantConnection(int[][] edges) {
        int n = edges.length;
        parent = new int[n + 1];
        rank = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) parent[i] = i;

        for (int[] edge : edges) {
            int u = edge[0], v = edge[1];
            if (find(u) == find(v)) return edge; // cycle detected
            union(u, v);
        }
        return new int[0]; // should not reach here
    }

    private int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); // path compression
        return parent[x];
    }

    private void union(int a, int b) {
        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
        if (ra == rb) return;
        if (rank[ra] < rank[rb]) parent[ra] = rb;
        else if (rank[ra] > rank[rb]) parent[rb] = ra;
        else { parent[rb] = ra; rank[ra]++; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] edges = {{1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}};
        int[] result = new RedundantConnection().findRedundantConnection(edges);
        System.out.println("Redundant edge: [" + result[0] + ", " + result[1] + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
Redundant edge: [2, 3]
```

### Why does Union-Find detect the redundant edge?

Union-Find tracks which nodes are in the same connected component. For each edge, we check whether both endpoints already belong to the same component by comparing their roots. If they do, adding this edge would create a cycle because a path already exists between them. If they do not, we union them. The first edge that fails the union check is the one that closes the cycle.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhyUnionFind {
    // Edge processing in order: {1,2}, {1,3}, {2,3}
    //
    // After {1,2}: components {1,2} and {3}
    // After {1,3}: components {1,2,3}
    // Edge {2,3}: find(2)==find(3) => already connected => REDUNDANT

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Union-Find detects the edge that closes a cycle.");
        System.out.println("If find(u) == find(v) before union, the edge is redundant.");
    }
}
```

### How do path compression and union by rank work?

Path compression flattens the tree during `find` operations by making every node point directly to the root. Union by rank always attaches the shorter tree under the taller one. Together they make each operation nearly O(1), specifically O(alpha(n)) where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PathCompressionDemo {
    // Without path compression: find can be O(n) in a degenerate chain
    // With path compression: find flattens the tree, subsequent finds are O(1)

    // Without union by rank: tree can degenerate into a linked list
    // With union by rank: tree stays balanced, height stays O(log n)

    static int[] parent = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    static int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); // path compression
        return parent[x];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Chain: 5 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1
        parent[5] = 4; parent[4] = 3; parent[3] = 2; parent[2] = 1;
        System.out.println("Before: parent[5]=" + parent[5]);
        System.out.println("find(5) = " + find(5));
        System.out.println("After compression: parent[5]=" + parent[5]); // now points to 1
    }
}
```

```text
Before: parent[5]=4
find(5) = 1
After compression: parent[5]=1
```

### How is this different from cycle detection with DFS?

| Feature | Union-Find | DFS cycle detection |
|---|---|---|
| Edge processing | one edge at a time, incremental | full graph traversal |
| Data structure | parent array + rank array | adjacency list + visited set |
| Returns | the specific edge causing the cycle | whether a cycle exists (boolean) |
| Time | O(E * alpha(V)) near-linear | O(V + E) |
| Best for | incremental edge addition | static graph |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class UnionFindVsDFS {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Union-Find: processes edges incrementally, returns the edge.");
        System.out.println("DFS: traverses full graph, returns boolean.");
        System.out.println("For this problem, Union-Find is the right choice.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a graph with only two nodes and two edges between them (the second edge is redundant), a graph where the redundant edge is the very first edge (a self-loop, though the problem typically excludes self-loops), and a graph where the cycle involves all n nodes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RedundantEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RedundantConnection solver = new RedundantConnection();

        // Two nodes, two edges: second is redundant
        int[][] twoNodes = {{1, 2}, {2, 1}};
        int[] r = solver.findRedundantConnection(twoNodes);
        System.out.println("Two nodes: [" + r[0] + ", " + r[1] + "]");

        // Cycle involving all nodes
        int[][] fullCycle = {{1, 2}, {2, 3}, {3, 4}, {4, 1}};
        r = new RedundantConnection().findRedundantConnection(fullCycle);
        System.out.println("Full cycle: [" + r[0] + ", " + r[1] + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
Two nodes: [2, 1]
Full cycle: [4, 1]
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to initialise each node's parent to itself. Without initialisation, all parents default to 0, and find incorrectly treats all nodes as connected. Another mistake is not implementing path compression, which makes find O(n) in the worst case.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: parent array not initialised
int[] parent = new int[n + 1]; // all zeros by default
// find(1) returns 0, find(2) returns 0 => looks like they share a root
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: initialise each node as its own parent
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) parent[i] = i;
// find(1) returns 1, find(2) returns 2 => correctly separate
```

### When should you use Union-Find for cycle detection?

Use Union-Find for cycle detection when edges are processed incrementally and you need to identify the specific edge that creates a cycle. It is also the right tool when you need to count connected components as edges are added. For a static graph where you only need a boolean answer, DFS is simpler.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhenToUseUnionFind {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Use Union-Find when:");
        System.out.println("  1. Edges arrive incrementally.");
        System.out.println("  2. Need the specific cycle-causing edge.");
        System.out.println("  3. Need connected component count over time.");
        System.out.println("Use DFS when:");
        System.out.println("  1. Graph is static.");
        System.out.println("  2. Only need boolean cycle detection.");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that this problem generalises to incremental cycle detection in any undirected graph, not just trees. They also know that the directed variant (Redundant Connection II) is significantly harder because it requires tracking in-degree-two conflicts and handling both cycles and multiple-parent nodes. In production at National Bank of India, the settlement topology validator uses Union-Find to check that each new link does not create a loop in the settlement tree.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorRedundantNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior insights:");
        System.out.println("  1. Generalises to any undirected incremental cycle detection.");
        System.out.println("  2. Directed variant (II) tracks in-degree-two + cycles.");
        System.out.println("  3. Union-Find with path compression + rank: O(E * alpha(V)).");
        System.out.println("  4. Production use: settlement topology validation.");
    }
}
```
